1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,421
A married woman laughs, kisses her husband, and says: Oh, I have overcome everything!

2
00:00:06,421 --> 00:00:08,700
This time I shall be very nice to him.

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:12,457
Readers have argued about that sentence for a hundred and thirty years.

4
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:15,025
Has she overcome the temptation?

5
00:00:15,025 --> 00:00:17,590
Or has she overcome her resistance to it?

6
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:23,717
By the end of this lesson, you will be able to argue both, and say why the writer chose not to tell us.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:35,115
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 2: A Respectable Woman, by Kate Chopin.

8
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:38,634
Six parts.

9
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:41,213
Kate Chopin and her Louisiana.

10
00:00:41,213 --> 00:00:42,241
The story.

11
00:00:42,241 --> 00:00:43,660
The night on the bench.

12
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:44,871
The characters.

13
00:00:44,871 --> 00:00:46,069
The themes.

14
00:00:46,069 --> 00:00:48,786
And the ending, with the craft that makes it work.

15
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:57,327
Kate Chopin was born in eighteen fifty in St. Louis.

16
00:00:57,327 --> 00:01:05,661
Her father died when she was five, and she grew up in a house run by women: her mother, grandmother and great grandmother.

17
00:01:05,661 --> 00:01:12,861
She married and moved to Louisiana, was widowed in eighteen eighty two, and began writing in her late thirties.

18
00:01:12,861 --> 00:01:17,262
This story first appeared in Vogue magazine in eighteen ninety four.

19
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:29,582
Her novel The Awakening, about a wife who leaves her husband, was attacked as indecent in eighteen ninety nine, and her career never recovered, until critics rediscovered her in the twentieth century.

20
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:44,096
Chopin made her name as a local colour writer, recording one region's speech and customs: the Creole sugar country of Louisiana, with its pillared porches, river banks, and French phrases.

21
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:47,872
But in her best stories the region is only the surface.

22
00:01:47,872 --> 00:01:53,141
Her real subject is the inner life of women, and the roles their society hands them.

23
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:08,000
The story follows the classic shape exactly: exposition, rising action, a climax on the bench, falling action, and a resolution.

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,317
What is unusual is that every stage happens inside her mind, and the resolution resolves nothing.

25
00:02:15,317 --> 00:02:16,217
Let us walk along it.

26
00:02:18,433 --> 00:02:28,604
Mrs. Baroda is a little provoked: her husband Gaston has invited an old college friend, Gouvernail, a journalist, to stay on their sugar plantation.

27
00:02:28,604 --> 00:02:31,950
She wanted rest, and her husband to herself.

28
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:42,498
She has never met the man, but she imagines him, tall, cynical, in eyeglasses, and on that evidence, which is no evidence at all, she dislikes him.

29
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:50,811
The real Gouvernail is nothing like her picture, and to her surprise she rather likes him, without knowing why.

30
00:02:50,811 --> 00:02:56,254
He is quiet and perfectly polite, but he makes no effort at all to win her approval.

31
00:02:56,254 --> 00:02:57,465
He simply does not need it.

32
00:02:58,667 --> 00:02:59,840
He wants nothing.

33
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:04,111
He sits on the portico, smoking, saying this is what I call living.

34
00:03:04,111 --> 00:03:08,708
He makes friends with the dogs and has no wish to fish or shoot.

35
00:03:08,708 --> 00:03:16,637
A hostess whose skill is supplying what guests want has nothing to do, and she is piqued: her vanity is stung.

36
00:03:16,637 --> 00:03:19,372
She leaves him alone; he does not mind.

37
00:03:19,372 --> 00:03:23,283
So she forces her company on him, trying to break through his reserve.

38
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,434
She complains to Gaston that the friend tires her frightfully.

39
00:03:28,434 --> 00:03:31,780
Gaston is puzzled: the man gives her no trouble.

40
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:38,134
I should like him better if he did, she answers, a reply that gives away more than she knows.

41
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:41,066
She announces she will go to the city in the morning.

42
00:03:42,267 --> 00:03:48,297
That night she sits alone on a bench under a live oak, more confused than she has ever been.

43
00:03:48,297 --> 00:03:55,394
A red point of light approaches: a lighted cigar, so it must be Gouvernail, because her husband does not smoke.

44
00:03:55,394 --> 00:03:59,773
He sits beside her, and in the dark his reserve melts, and he talks.

45
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,939
She tells nobody, takes the early train to the city, and does not come back until he has left.

46
00:04:06,939 --> 00:04:10,923
When Gaston wants him back in summer, she strongly objects.

47
00:04:10,923 --> 00:04:15,917
Then, before the year is out, she herself proposes that Gouvernail visit again.

48
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:25,543
In the dark, a red cigar point comes up the gravel walk.

49
00:04:25,543 --> 00:04:34,892
Gouvernail sits down, and half to himself he quotes Walt Whitman: night of south winds, night of the large few stars.

50
00:04:34,892 --> 00:04:42,535
Whitman was America's great poet of the body and of open desire, so the quotation says what the characters cannot.

51
00:04:42,535 --> 00:04:51,671
Then he talks of his old ambitions, and of what is left: only a wish to exist, with now and then a little whiff of genuine life.

52
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:57,680
Her mind barely follows his words; she is drinking in his voice.

53
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:07,498
She wants to touch his face in the dark, to whisper against his cheek, as she might have done, Chopin writes, if she had not been a respectable woman.

54
00:05:07,498 --> 00:05:16,089
Then the sentence on which the story turns: the stronger the impulse grew to bring herself near him, the further did she draw away.

55
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:24,531
That is antithesis: one sentence that holds the whole conflict, desire and restraint pulling in opposite directions.

56
00:05:24,531 --> 00:05:26,236
She gets up and leaves.

57
00:05:26,236 --> 00:05:29,953
Gouvernail, completely unaware, lights another cigar.

58
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:36,234
She thinks of telling her husband, who is also her friend, and decides not to.

59
00:05:36,234 --> 00:05:45,228
Besides being a respectable woman, she was a very sensible one, and she knew there are some battles in life which a human being must fight alone.

60
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,408
Three characters.

61
00:05:51,408 --> 00:06:03,778
Mrs. Baroda is not wicked and not weak; she has simply never met a situation her training could not handle, and notice that it begins in vanity: she is piqued at being ignored.

62
00:06:03,778 --> 00:06:08,869
Gouvernail is the catalyst who does nothing at all, which is exactly the point.

63
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:16,994
Gaston is warm, frank and trusting: the safe marriage, and its blindness, since he never learns a thing.

64
00:06:16,994 --> 00:06:24,927
Even objects speak: the red cigar in the dark, her white gown that gives her away, and the white scarf her husband sends out.

65
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:30,847
Four themes.

66
00:06:30,847 --> 00:06:40,965
Desire against respectability: respectable is a social word, about how a woman is seen, and what stops her is an identity, not her conscience.

67
00:06:40,965 --> 00:06:48,217
Chopin's quiet boldness is to show the impulse as natural and the restraint as coming from outside.

68
00:06:48,217 --> 00:06:53,768
Second, the inner life is the real life: outwardly, a guest comes and goes.

69
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,483
Third, self knowledge: she learns three things she never knew about herself.

70
00:06:59,483 --> 00:07:08,832
Fourth, marriage and its limits: a warm, loving marriage can still leave a woman completely alone with the thing that matters most.

71
00:07:08,832 --> 00:07:14,574
This awakening is what Chopin would expand, five years later, into a whole novel.

72
00:07:19,367 --> 00:07:20,462
Now the ending.

73
00:07:20,462 --> 00:07:22,962
Reading one: virtue has won.

74
00:07:22,962 --> 00:07:27,103
She has mastered the feeling, so she can safely invite him back.

75
00:07:27,103 --> 00:07:34,225
Reading two: what she has overcome is her resistance, and being very nice to him is a promise.

76
00:07:34,225 --> 00:07:41,790
Reading three: Chopin arranged the words so that both fit, and handed the moral judgement to us.

77
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:48,112
In the exam, give the readings, the evidence for each, and say why the ending is left open.

78
00:07:49,333 --> 00:07:50,962
The devices to quote.

79
00:07:50,962 --> 00:07:57,408
Situational irony: she dislikes the man she has never met and is drawn to the man who ignores her.

80
00:07:57,408 --> 00:07:59,113
Free indirect style.

81
00:07:59,113 --> 00:08:01,262
The antithesis on the bench.

82
00:08:01,262 --> 00:08:02,655
Allusion to Whitman.

83
00:08:02,655 --> 00:08:06,158
Euphemism: she calls her desire only a folly.

84
00:08:06,158 --> 00:08:10,350
Foreshadowing: I should like him better if he did give me trouble.

85
00:08:10,350 --> 00:08:11,265
And the open ending.

86
00:08:13,667 --> 00:08:14,437
Pause and think.

87
00:08:21,733 --> 00:08:27,843
He makes no appeal for her approval; her vanity is piqued long before anything like love.

88
00:08:29,833 --> 00:08:30,268
One more.

89
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:44,336
Antithesis: opposing ideas balanced in one sentence — desire against restraint.

90
00:08:46,533 --> 00:08:47,602
Quick recap.

91
00:08:47,602 --> 00:08:50,128
Chopin, and her Louisiana.

92
00:08:50,128 --> 00:08:55,805
A guest disliked before he arrives, and unsettling because he wants nothing.

93
00:08:55,805 --> 00:09:01,287
The night on the bench, where the stronger the impulse, the further she draws away.

94
00:09:01,287 --> 00:09:03,566
Desire against respectability.

95
00:09:03,566 --> 00:09:05,734
And an ending that can be read two ways.

96
00:09:09,933 --> 00:09:11,784
That was A Respectable Woman.

97
00:09:11,784 --> 00:09:19,518
Next: A Devoted Son, by Anita Desai, a story of a perfect son and a father who wants a sweet.

98
00:09:19,518 --> 00:09:20,073
See you there.

